         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "11576d79-3be9-415c-bce0-616b4eba4524",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "party",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "4a65633e-9907-4ee1-92b1-091f879064d8",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=4a65633e-9907-4ee1-92b1-091f879064d8",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Conclusion\n\nIn this class, we learned what a multiplication table is: an organized set of results, built by adding the same number many times.\n\nWe also learned how we are going to mount them in the next classes: each table repeats its number **10 times** — one repeat for each value from 0 to 9 — and beyond this limit we can continue multiplying, because the order of the values never changes the result.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
